---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import StudyHeader from "../../components/study/StudyHeader.astro";
import Act from "../../components/study/Act.astro";
import DecisionCard from "../../components/study/DecisionCard.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/study/Figure.astro";
import EndBlock from "../../components/study/EndBlock.astro";
import PrototypeCallout from "../../components/study/PrototypeCallout.astro";
import { pitchPrototype, shippingPilotPrototype } from "../../data/prototypes";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import screenAdminOverview from "../../assets/ai-commerce/screen-01-admin-overview.png";
import screenAgentRfqDraft from "../../assets/ai-commerce/screen-02-agent-rfq-draft.png";
import screenImportReview from "../../assets/ai-commerce/screen-03-import-review.png";
import screenAgentRegistry from "../../assets/ai-commerce/screen-04-agent-registry.png";
import "../../styles/study.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="SusTern AI-Commerce — the work around a product — Danilo Boquet"
  description="Designing SusTern AI-Commerce: product information, branded storefronts, and connected workflows for specialty businesses — with human review where trust matters."
>
  <StudyHeader
    num="01"
    title="SusTern AI-Commerce"
    hook="The work around a product matters as much as the product itself. SusTern AI-Commerce connects product information, branded storefronts, and connected workflows for specialty businesses — retailers, distributors, and manufacturers."
    meta={[
      { label: "Role", value: "Product & UX design lead" },
      { label: "Company", value: "SusTern" },
      { label: "Timeline", value: "2025 — present" },
      { label: "Scope", value: "Platform concept, flows, design system, strategy" },
    ]}
    outcome="Pitched the platform, designed the system — one catalog for retail and wholesale; storefronts, admin, and the network shipped."
  />

  <Act num="01" title="The scene: the work around a product">
    <p class="lede">
      A specialty furniture retailer has outgrown template commerce. Their catalog doesn't fit
      the boxes — variants, bundles, supplier relationships, lot tracking. Their business is two
      businesses: retail customers on the storefront, distributors on the wholesale side, both
      needing the same inventory truth. And the team running it all is two or three people.
    </p>
    <p>
      Around every product, more people: a customer reorders by texting a photo of a chair; a
      supplier answers a quote on a call; a receiver confirms a delivery by replying "YES." None
      of them have an app installed, a login, or a minute of training.
    </p>
    <p>
      SusTern AI-Commerce is the system behind all of it. Product information is the source of
      truth, branded storefronts are the public face, and connected workflows are how the work
      gets done — with human review wherever trust matters.
    </p>
    <p>
      The platform began as a proposal. I joined SusTern to work on its first product, noryX —
      and the more I saw, the more potential I found. I brought the first version of the
      AI-Commerce platform to the team to make the case that we could do so much more. It landed:
      an exceptional small team, some strikingly good ideas, and a founder willing to bet on
      them. My job since has been to design the system: its flows, its language, and the moments
      where AI proposals become human decisions.
    </p>
    {
      pitchPrototype.enabled && (
        <p class="proto-inline">
          The pitch came with a working prototype —{" "}
          <a
            href={pitchPrototype.href}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
            aria-describedby="pitch-prototype-note"
          >
            open the original build I used to make the case<span aria-hidden="true"> ↗</span>
          </a>
          <span class="proto-note" id="pitch-prototype-note">
            {" "}
            (Figma Make prototype · demo data · opens in a new tab)
          </span>
        </p>
      )
    }
  </Act>

  <Act num="02" title="The constraints">
    <ul class="constraints">
      <li>
        <strong>Many businesses, one product.</strong>
        <span>
          One storefront codebase and one admin serve many merchant brands. Identity has to flex
          without forking the platform.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>B2B and B2C, one model.</strong>
        <span> Wholesale and retail are the same catalog, the same stock, the same money. </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No app. No training. No login.</strong>
        <span>
          Floor workers, suppliers, and customers interact through voice, SMS, and signed links —
          the channels they already live in. If a flow needs an install or a manual, it's designed
          wrong.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AI as an actor — with a human at the commit.</strong>
        <span>
          The system drafts, classifies, and proposes. People approve. Every automated step has to
          be inspectable, because operators are the ones accountable for what the system does.
        </span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Act>

  <Act num="03" title="The decisions">
    <DecisionCard
      title="Channels people already use become product surfaces"
      bet="Treat voice, SMS, and links as first-class interfaces: product photos in by MMS, order summaries and payment links out over SMS, registration gated by a one-time code during a call. The phone line isn't support — it's a storefront."
      alternative="A companion app with an onboarding funnel, and “call us” as the fallback path for everyone else."
      why="Every install is a wall, and training doesn't survive staff turnover. Designing for the channels people already carry means adoption is never a prerequisite — the interface is already in their pocket. And the same intents flow into the same backend as the admin, so the business keeps one version of the truth."
    />
    <DecisionCard
      title="One workspace, scoped to context"
      bet="A single admin shell where you select the client namespace and the facility — and see only what your scope allows. Requirements appear as guidance (“requires a namespace, facility, and active grant”), not as disabled walls."
      alternative="Separate tools or skins per persona — provider, merchant, manufacturer."
      why="A small team can't maintain parallel products. One design language means one pattern library, one set of fixes, and screens that stay simple because context — not extra code — decides what's shown."
    />
    <DecisionCard
      title="One catalog, both buying modes"
      bet="Model the catalog once — variants, volume pricing, supplier relationships, lot tracking — and let pricing, availability, and packaging rules differ by buyer group."
      alternative="A separate B2B portal bolted onto a B2C store."
      why="Specialty retail lives on both sides of that line; splitting the model splits the truth. One catalog keeps stock honest and lets a merchant quote a distributor from the same screen they sell from."
    />
    <DecisionCard
      title="AI drafts; the operator holds the pen"
      bet="AI generates and proposes — product descriptions, SEO, campaigns, imports, restock suggestions — but consequential steps resolve to an explicit human decision, with sources and reasoning one reveal away."
      alternative="Full automation for speed, or a chat box bolted onto the side of the product."
      why="Small teams defend every choice they ship. Automation earns trust by being visible: the draft is a draft, the proposal explains itself, and the human keeps the pen."
    />
    <div class="rejected">
      <p class="rejected-head">What we said no to</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A companion app — anything that needs installing or training</li>
        <li>A separate B2B portal — splitting the catalog would split the truth</li>
        <li>Silent automation — full auto-apply with no human at the commit</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Act>

  <Act num="04" title="The work">
    <p class="study-disclosure">
      Figures below reconstruct shipped patterns from the design documentation and are labelled
      “Reconstruction.” Sanitized product captures are added as they clear review.
    </p>
    <div class="fig-grid fig-grid--2">
      <Figure
        label="Reconstruction"
        caption="From channel to action: voice, SMS, and photo intake all resolve into the same pipeline — captured, drafted, confirmed."
      >
        <div class="fx">
          <div class="fx-bar">
            <span class="fx-dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span>
            <span class="fx-title">Intake pipeline</span>
          </div>
          <div class="fx-body">
            <div class="pipe">
              <div class="pipe-node">
                <span class="pipe-node-label">Channel</span>
                <div class="pipe-node-title">Voice · SMS · MMS</div>
              </div>
              <span class="pipe-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
              <div class="pipe-node">
                <span class="pipe-node-label">Captured</span>
                <div class="pipe-node-title">Intent + items</div>
              </div>
              <span class="pipe-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
              <div class="pipe-node">
                <span class="pipe-node-label">Drafted</span>
                <div class="pipe-node-title">AI proposes the action</div>
              </div>
              <span class="pipe-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
              <div class="pipe-node">
                <span class="pipe-node-label">Confirmed</span>
                <div class="pipe-node-title">Human decides</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </Figure>
      <Figure
        label="Reconstruction"
        caption="A reorder handled entirely in a text thread — photo in, order summary and payment link out. No app, no login."
      >
        <div class="fx">
          <div class="fx-bar">
            <span class="fx-dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span>
            <span class="fx-title">SMS — customer line</span>
          </div>
          <div class="fx-body">
            <div class="sms-thread">
              <span class="sms-day">Tuesday 9:41 AM</span>
              <div class="bubble bubble-in">
                Can I reorder the walnut dining set from last spring?
                <div class="sms-photo">[ photo ] walnut-chair.jpg — sent by customer</div>
                <div class="sms-meta">Customer · 9:41 AM</div>
              </div>
              <div class="bubble bubble-out">
                Of course — 1× Walnut Dining Set, delivered to your usual address.
                <span class="sms-link">Secure payment link</span>
                <div class="sms-meta">SusTern · 9:42 AM</div>
              </div>
              <div class="bubble bubble-in">
                Order placed — thank you!
                <div class="sms-meta">Customer · 9:44 AM</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </Figure>
      <Figure
        label="Sanitized capture"
        caption="The admin overview — one workspace for the whole business: top products, inventory health, recent orders, and the assistant in reach."
        pad={false}
        zoom
      >
        <Image
          src={screenAdminOverview}
          alt="Merchant admin command center: top-products chart, customer pulse, inventory health, recent orders with a status donut, and the assistant panel open"
          widths={[640, 960, 1874]}
          sizes="(max-width: 720px) 92vw, 500px"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </Figure>
      <Figure
        label="Sanitized capture"
        caption="An AI proposal in context — the agent reads the conversation and drafts the RFQ with its summary; the operator decides in the thread: Review & Send or Discard."
        pad={false}
        zoom
      >
        <Image
          src={screenAgentRfqDraft}
          alt="CRM relationship inbox with a bulk-order thread: an agent summary, an agent-created RFQ draft with Review & Send and Discard actions, and the customer message that triggered it"
          widths={[640, 960, 1604]}
          sizes="(max-width: 720px) 92vw, 500px"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </Figure>
      <Figure
        label="Sanitized capture"
        caption="Eleven agents, one approval model — the registry keeps every automation a named actor: low-risk tasks run pre-approved, higher-stake decisions wait for the operator."
        pad={false}
        zoom
      >
        <Image
          src={screenAgentRegistry}
          alt="Agentic commerce automation, Workflows & Agents: a semi-auto automation-mode banner with approval required for high-stake, above a registry of eleven agent cards spanning procurement, catalog, storefront, operations, and CRM"
          widths={[640, 960, 1604]}
          sizes="(max-width: 720px) 92vw, 500px"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </Figure>
      <Figure
        label="Sanitized capture"
        caption="Import in the admin — the AI drafts every row with field-level confidence; nothing commits until the operator reviews."
        pad={false}
        zoom
      >
        <Image
          src={screenImportReview}
          alt="Import BOM components review: a CSV import summary with 57 pending draft rows, each field carrying a confidence chip, committed rows marked, and Commit all and Reject all controls"
          widths={[640, 960, 1525]}
          sizes="(max-width: 720px) 92vw, 500px"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </Figure>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top: 28px">
      The network behind the catalog: the platform's public face is its showcase portal, where
      manufacturers, distributors, and buyers present their products and find each other. Product
      information is the first sales channel — the storefront is the context layer that turns
      browsing into a conversation.
    </p>
    <p class="group-label">How this shipped</p>
    <div class="shipped">
      <div class="shipped-step">
        <span class="shipped-step-label">Audit</span>
        <p>Every surface reviewed against one design language as the platform grew</p>
      </div>
      <div class="shipped-step">
        <span class="shipped-step-label">Decide</span>
        <p>One shell, one catalog, one vocabulary — alternatives documented</p>
      </div>
      <div class="shipped-step">
        <span class="shipped-step-label">Ship</span>
        <p>Storefronts, admin, and network shipped without forking the product</p>
      </div>
      <div class="shipped-step">
        <span class="shipped-step-label">Govern</span>
        <p>The pattern library versioned; new surfaces assemble from canonical parts</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Act>

  <Act num="05" title="Impact & status">
    <p>
      Shipped and evolving. Merchant storefronts run on the platform today — catalog to checkout —
      with the admin behind them and the showcase portal connecting manufacturers, distributors,
      and buyers. Recent work rarely comes with public metrics at this stage, so here's what I'd
      point to instead: one catalog model now serves retail and wholesale without divergence; the
      no-training constraint held all the way to production; and the admin stayed one system as
      the surface count grew.
    </p>
    <div class="counted">
      <div class="counted-item">
        <span class="counted-num">1</span>
        <span class="counted-label">admin shell, every role</span>
      </div>
      <div class="counted-item">
        <span class="counted-num">1</span>
        <span class="counted-label">catalog, both buying modes</span>
      </div>
      <div class="counted-item">
        <span class="counted-num">0</span>
        <span class="counted-label">per-merchant forks</span>
      </div>
      <div class="counted-item">
        <span class="counted-num">4</span>
        <span class="counted-label">workflow stages, one system</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      The polished marketing version of the platform lives at its showcase portal; this is the
      design version.
    </p>
    {
      shippingPilotPrototype.enabled && (
        <PrototypeCallout
          id="shipping-pilot"
          eyebrow="Side-project pilot"
          title="Delivery services, inside the platform"
          description="A side-project designed to extend the platform into delivery: a shipping-provider portal — last-mile orders, AI route planning, fleet management, delivery KPIs — piloted in the Kitchener–Waterloo–Cambridge area with a local delivery co-operative. I designed and built the pilot prototype; demo data throughout."
          href={shippingPilotPrototype.href}
          cta="Open the pilot prototype"
          note="Figma Make build (AI-assisted) · demo data · opens in a new tab · best on a desktop"
        />
      )
    }
  </Act>

  <Act num="06" title="What I'd do differently">
    <p>
      I'd push the pattern library even earlier. We committed to a governed design system a few
      months in; every week before it was paid for in inconsistency I had to clean up later.
    </p>
    <p>
      And I'd sequence catalog ingestion sooner. We designed the storefront first because it's
      visible — but the import pipeline is where merchant trust is actually won. Bad data makes
      every screen a lie.
    </p>
  </Act>

  <EndBlock nextTitle="SusTern TPL Platform" nextHref="/work/tpl-platform/" />
</BaseLayout>
